      "math_academy_id": "e-735",
      "problem": "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
